{"availability":"available","full":"reactjs/react-gradual-upgrade-demo","name":"react-gradual-upgrade-demo","description":"Demonstration of how to gradually upgrade an app to a new version of React","category":"Developer tools","language":"JavaScript","stars":416,"forks":28,"created":"2020-08-07T12:56:21Z","updated":"2026-03-29T13:25:18Z","license":"MIT","branch":"main","homepage":"https://reactjs.org/blog/2020/08/10/react-v17-rc.html","topics":["gradual-upgrades","react"],"demo":"https://next-gradual-upgrade-demo.vercel.app/","color":"#25392f","ink":"#d1e5c5","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-15T21:46:58.107Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-15T21:46:58.107Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-15T21:46:58.107Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-06T01:25:16.349Z","stars":417,"forks":28},{"at":"2026-10-08T04:50:15.228Z","stars":417,"forks":28},{"at":"2026-10-10T05:45:40.898Z","stars":416,"forks":28},{"at":"2026-10-11T20:54:00.689Z","stars":416,"forks":28},{"at":"2026-10-15T21:46:58.107Z","stars":416,"forks":28}],"technologies":["React"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["This is a demo of how you can configure a build system to serve two different versions of React side by side in the same app. This is not optimal, and should only be used as a compromise to prevent your app from getting stuck on an old version of React.","Note that this approach is meant to be an escape hatch, not the norm.","Normally, we encourage you to use a single version of React across your whole app. When you need to upgrade React, it is better to try to upgrade it all at once. We try to keep breaking changes between versions to the minimum, and often there are automatic scripts (\"codemods\") that can assist you with migration. You can always find the migration information for any release on our blog."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/reactjs/react-gradual-upgrade-demo/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"763f506b3b9bf309e44695bb746fd3a4bfc1d4eb344aeb8e713ef31e818a361e","extractedAt":"2026-1